WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.707
Test without a model first.

2
00:00:01.987 --> 00:00:06.488
Checks and balances start before any model sees your server, in two layers.

3
00:00:06.768 --> 00:00:08.347
Unit tests on the logic.

4
00:00:08.627 --> 00:00:23.304
Because the tracker logic has no MCP imports, ordinary pytest tests cover it: validation rejects bad ids, the preview changes nothing, a confirmed change writes one history entry, a repeat is a no-op.

5
00:00:23.584 --> 00:00:27.232
These are the lab’s offline tests, and they run in milliseconds.

6
00:00:27.512 --> 00:00:29.261
In-memory protocol tests.

7
00:00:29.541 --> 00:00:40.720
The SDK’s Client connects to a URL, launches a subprocess, or, if you pass it your server object, talks to it directly in memory, with no process and no port.

8
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.605
The SDK docs compare it to FastAPI’s test client.

9
00:00:44.885 --> 00:00:54.571
A test opens Client(server, raise_exceptions=True), lists tools, calls them, reads resources, and gets prompts, asserting on the results.

10
00:00:54.851 --> 00:01:04.387
The raise_exceptions flag makes failures outside a tool body show their real message in your test instead of the sanitized one a remote caller would get.

11
00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:14.800
This layer catches what unit tests cannot: a schema the SDK generated differently than you expected, an annotation you forgot, a description that is empty.

12
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:24.637
The in-memory client is era-neutral by default, probing the server and choosing the protocol path, so the same test exercises what a current host would do.

13
00:01:24.917 --> 00:01:32.576
That matters because the most common MCP bugs are not in your logic; they are in what the host and model actually see.
